  "worked_solution": "The horizontal asymptote is $y={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is $x.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2{x}^{2}+x-1}{3x+2} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{x}+\\frac{x}{x}-\\frac{1}{x})}{(\\frac{3x}{x}+\\frac{2}{x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(2x+1-\\frac{1}{x})}{(3+\\frac{2}{x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(2x+1-0)}{(3+0)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(2x+1) \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe limit does not come out to a finite number. Therefore, there is no horizontal asymptote.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
